From: Oleg Tolmatcev <oleg.tolmatcev@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] feat(windows): rm and cat builtins

Configure scripts run rm -f and cat over a thousand times; on Windows
each process start costs about 4 ms, a sixth of gzip's configure.  As
bash's loadable rm and cat builtins do, the shell now handles them
itself: rm with -f, -r, -R and --, cat with files, - and --.  Other
options, operands that cannot be removed or opened, and "", ".", ".."
and drive operands go to the external program, so its messages and
exit status apply.  The builtins exist on Windows only; enable -n
turns them off.

